(window.SAT_DATA.mocks = window.SAT_DATA.mocks || []).push(
{"id": "mock-2", "title": "Hard Module B", "subtitle": "Modeled on the harder second module (Module 2) of the digital SAT R&W section", "timeLimitSeconds": 1920, "questions": [{"id": "rw-wic-e1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "For decades, the prevailing account held that Ancestral Puebloan communities abandoned the cliff dwellings of the Mesa Verde region in a single abrupt exodus late in the thirteenth century. Newly analyzed tree-ring dates from construction beams, however, have led archaeologist Dana Whitfield to ______ that account: she accepts that a final, rapid departure occurred but contends that it merely concluded decades of gradual, household-by-household emigration."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "corroborate", "why": "Reversed: \"however\" signals that the new tree-ring dates push against the prevailing account, and Whitfield revises rather than confirms it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "repudiate", "why": "Too absolute for the context: the colon shows she \"accepts that a final, rapid departure occurred,\" so she cannot be rejecting the account wholesale.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "qualify", "why": "In its secondary sense, to \"qualify\" a claim is to limit or modify it without discarding it—exactly the move elaborated after the colon: she accepts the exodus \"but contends that it merely concluded\" a longer, gradual process."}, {"text": "formulate", "why": "Illogical: the account has prevailed \"for decades\" and is not Whitfield's creation; the sentence describes her response to an existing view, not its invention.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-wic-e2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In the process linguists call grammaticalization, content words gradually harden into grammatical machinery: the English verb \"go,\" which once denoted physical motion alone, now signals futurity in constructions like \"it is going to rain.\" As words assume such grammatical duties, their original meanings are typically ______—the sense of motion in \"going to\" survives only as a faint trace, imposing no requirement that anything actually go anywhere."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "attenuated", "why": "\"Attenuated\" means weakened or thinned out, matching the elaboration after the dash that the motion sense \"survives only as a faint trace\"—diminished yet not destroyed."}, {"text": "eradicated", "why": "Intensity mismatch: eradication means complete elimination, but the text insists that a \"faint trace\" of the original meaning still survives.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "amplified", "why": "Reversed: the dash describes a meaning that has faded to a trace, not one that has grown stronger as the word took on grammatical duties.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "transposed", "why": "Nothing in the text describes meanings exchanging places or shifting position; the change described is a loss of force, not a relocation.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-wic-e3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from Edith Wharton's 1904 short story \"The Other Two.\" Waythorn's wife was twice divorced before their marriage; circumstances keep drawing her former husbands, Haskett and Varick, into his affairs.\n\nWaythorn had once imagined that a man's life could be kept wholly his own; he was now daily more sensible of the ways in which the two men pressed upon his household. Each courteous visit, each unavoidable business dealing, left him with a smaller share of the privacy he had so confidently supposed himself to possess."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"sensible\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Reasonable", "why": "A genuine dictionary sense of \"sensible,\" but the construction \"sensible of the ways\" describes what Waythorn perceives, not the soundness of his judgment.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Perceptible", "why": "Reverses the relation: \"perceptible\" would describe the intrusions as capable of being noticed, whereas the text makes Waythorn the one doing the noticing (\"he was...sensible of\").", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Practical", "why": "Another common sense of \"sensible,\" but the text never evaluates Waythorn's pragmatism; it catalogs what he notices happening to his household.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Conscious", "why": "In the phrase \"sensible of,\" the word means aware: Waythorn grows \"daily more\" conscious of the men's encroachments, as the following inventory of visits and business dealings confirms."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-wic-e4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Whether the metalwork hoards buried across Bronze Age northern Europe were votive offerings or simply valuables concealed in moments of danger remains contested, largely because the material evidence is so often ______: a cache of axe-heads deposited at the margin of a bog is equally consistent with a ritual gift that was never meant to be recovered and with a smith's hidden stock that awaited a retrieval that never came."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "irrefutable", "why": "Reversed: evidence that could not be disputed would settle the question rather than leave it \"contested.\"", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "equivocal", "why": "\"Equivocal\" evidence admits more than one interpretation, matching the colon's elaboration that a single cache is \"equally consistent with\" a ritual offering and a hidden stock."}, {"text": "exiguous", "why": "The dispute is attributed to ambiguity, not scarcity: the colon's example concerns what one cache can mean, not how few caches exist.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "extraneous", "why": "The hoards are the central objects of the debate, so the evidence cannot be irrelevant to it; the problem is what it shows, not whether it pertains.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-wic-e5", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Analyzing interviews recorded with three generations of residents of a South Carolina sea island, sociolinguist Renata Osei observed that a handful of distinctive vowel pronunciations persisted even as most other features of the island's dialect faded from use. The pronunciations endure, she argues, because they serve as ______ of belonging: on hearing them, islanders instantly register that a speaker's roots lie within the community."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "an index", "why": "In its secondary sense, an \"index\" is a sign or indicator: the pronunciations function as signals from which islanders \"instantly register\" a speaker's local roots."}, {"text": "a vestige", "why": "Attractive because other dialect features have \"faded,\" but a vestige is an inert remnant, whereas the text explains the pronunciations' persistence by their active social function.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "an arbiter", "why": "An arbiter decides or judges; the pronunciations do not settle who belongs but merely allow listeners to recognize belonging.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "a facsimile", "why": "A facsimile is an exact copy, and nothing in the text presents the pronunciations as reproducing belonging or anything else.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-tsp-d3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "For much of the twentieth century, paleontologists regarded the earliest known bird, Archaeopteryx, as a straightforward intermediate: a creature poised neatly between small predatory dinosaurs and modern birds. Recent analyses have unsettled that tidy picture. As additional feathered dinosaur fossils have surfaced in China, several show plumage as elaborate as Archaeopteryx's yet belong to lineages only distantly related to birds. The very trait once thought to mark the origin of the bird lineage now appears to have arisen repeatedly among dinosaurs. Rather than a lone signpost on the road to birds, Archaeopteryx increasingly looks like one branch on a densely feathered bush."}, "stem": "Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "It describes a fossil discovery and then explains the methods paleontologists used to determine the fossil's age.", "why": "No dating methods are discussed; the text contrasts an older interpretation with newer evidence, not techniques for aging a specimen.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It outlines the traditional view of Archaeopteryx and then defends that view against recent challenges.", "why": "The text does the opposite of defending the traditional view: it says recent analyses \"unsettled that tidy picture\" and ends by revising it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It presents competing theories about the origin of feathers and then argues that the question remains unresolved.", "why": "No rival theories are laid out, and the closing sentence offers a revised conclusion rather than leaving the matter unresolved.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It presents a once-standard interpretation of a fossil and then describes evidence that has led scientists to revise it.", "why": "The text states the old reading (Archaeopteryx as a \"straightforward intermediate\"), introduces feathered dinosaur fossils that \"unsettled that tidy picture,\" and closes with the revised view (\"one branch on a densely feathered bush\")."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-tsp-d4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from an 1851 essay on the formation of character.\n\nThe young suppose that their present indulgences leave no lasting mark—that a fault taken up today may be as easily laid down tomorrow. Experience teaches otherwise. Every act we repeat wears its channel a little deeper, until at length the mind runs into it of its own accord, as water seeks the course it has long followed. [[u]]The stream that might once have been turned aside by a child's hand will in time cut a bed that no engineer can move.[[/u]] Thus the trifling habits of youth harden, almost unseen, into the fixed dispositions of the man."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It introduces a new argument about the training and expertise of engineers.", "why": "The engineer is figurative—a stand-in for any force strong enough to redirect the stream; the essay raises no actual argument about engineers.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "It extends the preceding image of flowing water to underscore how habits grow harder to alter over time.", "why": "The prior sentence introduces the water-channel image (\"as water seeks the course it has long followed\"); the underlined sentence carries it forward—from a stream a child could turn to a bed \"no engineer can move\"—to stress how fixed habits become."}, {"text": "It concedes that many youthful habits can still be corrected with sufficient effort.", "why": "The image stresses the opposite—the bed becomes one \"no engineer can move\"—so it emphasizes the difficulty of change, not the ease of correction.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It states the essay's central claim for the first time.", "why": "The central claim is already established earlier (\"Experience teaches otherwise\"; every act \"wears its channel a little deeper\"); the underlined sentence reinforces it through metaphor rather than introducing it.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-ctc-c4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "Economist Dana Whitfield contends that requiring chain restaurants to print calorie counts beside each menu item reduces excess eating. Once diners can see how many calories a dish contains, she argues, [[u]]they shift their orders toward lighter options, so the average number of calories purchased per meal declines[[/u]] wherever the labeling requirement takes effect.", "text2": "Analyzing sales records from before and after one such mandate, researcher Ibrahim Sole found that customers' selections scarcely changed. The calories listed on the menus fell all the same: restaurants had quietly reformulated many existing dishes—trimming portions and swapping ingredients—apparently to avoid advertising unflattering figures beside their most popular items."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Sole most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By concluding that calorie labeling ultimately does nothing to lower the calories contained in restaurant dishes.", "why": "Contradicts Text 2, which reports that 'the calories listed on the menus fell' after the mandate; labeling did lower them, just through reformulation.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By responding that the calorie counts dropped chiefly because restaurants reformulated their dishes, not because diners shifted their orders as Whitfield claims.", "why": "Text 2 finds 'customers' selections scarcely changed' while restaurants 'reformulated many existing dishes,' locating the effect in supply rather than in the consumer choices Text 1 posits."}, {"text": "By agreeing that diners moved toward lighter dishes but crediting the restaurants' advertising rather than the posted counts.", "why": "Accepts a shift in diners' orders that Text 2 explicitly did not observe, misrepresenting the study's central finding.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By warning that reformulating dishes to lower their calorie counts may leave them less flavorful for customers.", "why": "May be a reasonable worry, but flavor is irrelevant to Whitfield's claim about why calories purchased decline.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-cid-c4", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from Charles W. Chesnutt's 1898 short story \"The Wife of His Youth.\" The narrator is describing a social club.\n\nThe Blue Veins were a little society organized in a certain Northern city shortly after the Civil War. Its purpose was to establish and maintain correct social standards among a people whose social condition presented almost unlimited room for improvement. By accident, combined perhaps with some natural affinity, the society consisted of individuals who were, generally speaking, more white than black. Some envious outsider made the suggestion that no one was eligible for membership who was not white enough to show blue veins, and the suggestion was readily adopted by those who were not of the favored few; since that time the society, though possessing a longer and more pretentious name, had been known far and wide simply as the \"Blue Veins.\""}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "A social club organized after the Civil War formally restricted its membership to people whose skin was light enough to show blue veins.", "why": "The blue-vein test was \"the suggestion of some envious outsider\"—a gibe circulated by those excluded—not a formal requirement the society itself imposed, so this misattributes the outsiders' mockery to the club's rules.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "While a social club's stated purpose was to uphold correct social standards, the narrator implies that its membership was in practice shaped by an unacknowledged preference regarding complexion.", "why": "The narrator's arch aside that \"by accident, combined perhaps with some natural affinity\" the members were \"more white than black\" pointedly implies that complexion, not the professed standards, governed who belonged—the ironic gap the text is built around."}, {"text": "A social club's campaign to establish correct social standards substantially improved the social condition of the community around it.", "why": "The narrator wryly observes \"almost unlimited room for improvement\" but never reports that the society achieved any improvement at all.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "A social club acquired a mocking nickname that eventually displaced its longer and more pretentious official name.", "why": "True as a detail—the nickname did stick—but it is incidental to the text's central ironic point about what actually determined the society's membership.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-cid-c5", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Excavated Neolithic settlements in southeastern Europe routinely contain houses reduced to vitrified rubble, and for decades accidental fires were assumed to be the cause. Experimental archaeology has complicated that assumption: when researchers built and burned full-scale replicas of such houses, the fires died down well below the temperatures needed to vitrify clay walls unless the structures had been packed with extra fuel beforehand. Archaeologist Bogdana Ilić and colleagues therefore argue that many of the houses were destroyed deliberately, perhaps at the end of a household's life as a kind of ritual closure. Skeptics counter that the replicas' timber and thatch, grown and dried under modern conditions, may burn differently from prehistoric materials—so accident, in their view, cannot yet be ruled out."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Although burnings of replica houses support the hypothesis that Neolithic houses were set alight deliberately, the experiments have not conclusively excluded accidental fire as a cause.", "why": "The text presents the replica burnings as the basis on which Ilić and colleagues \"therefore argue\" for deliberate destruction, then closes with the skeptics' unanswered objection that modern materials \"may burn differently,\" so accident \"cannot yet be ruled out.\""}, {"text": "Full-scale replicas of Neolithic houses reach wall-vitrifying temperatures only when extra fuel is added to them before burning.", "why": "An accurate detail of the experimental results, but it functions as evidence within the larger dispute over deliberate versus accidental burning rather than stating the text's main idea.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Neolithic communities in southeastern Europe burned houses at the end of a household's life as a ritual of closure.", "why": "Ritual closure is offered only as a hedged possibility (\"perhaps\"); the text asserts neither the ritual motive nor deliberate burning as established fact.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Experimental archaeology is of limited value for studying prehistoric fires because modern building materials burn differently from prehistoric ones.", "why": "The skeptics say only that replica materials \"may burn differently\" in this case; the text draws no general conclusion about the value of experimental archaeology.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-cid-c6", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Incised signs appear on thousands of clay artifacts from the Vinča culture of Neolithic southeastern Europe; the signs recur across distant sites in stable forms, sometimes in sequences, and predate the earliest Mesopotamian writing by some two millennia. Whether they constitute writing is fiercely debated, but linguist Ana Marinković contends that the debate is largely definitional rather than empirical. Scholars who hold that writing must encode the sounds of speech reject the signs, since nothing suggests that they record any language; scholars who ask only that marks convey conventional meanings accept them. The corpus of artifacts, Marinković observes, has scarcely changed in decades, yet the signs' classification has flipped each time a new definition of writing has gained currency."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Because the Vinča signs predate the earliest Mesopotamian writing, the Vinča culture should be credited with producing the world's first writing system.", "why": "The text reports that the signs' status as writing is \"fiercely debated\"; crediting the Vinča culture with the first writing system presupposes the very classification that is in dispute.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Repeated discoveries of inscribed Vinča artifacts have forced scholars to revise their definitions of what counts as writing.", "why": "This reverses the text's closing point: the corpus \"has scarcely changed in decades,\" and it is shifting definitions that have flipped the signs' classification—not new discoveries driving definitional change.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "The signs on Vinča artifacts recur across distant sites in stable forms and were sometimes inscribed in sequences.", "why": "These properties are accurately reported, but they are background details about the corpus, not the text's central claim about why the debate over the signs persists.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Disagreement over whether the Vinča signs qualify as writing stems mainly from scholars' competing definitions of writing rather than from differing assessments of the evidence.", "why": "This states Marinković's contention that the debate is \"largely definitional rather than empirical,\" supported by the observation that an essentially unchanged corpus gets reclassified whenever a new definition of writing gains currency."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-cet-d3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "At a site in present-day Argentina, paleontologists documented more than a dozen parallel trackways left by a single sauropod species, all trending in the same direction. Diego Salgado and colleagues interpret the trackways as evidence that these sauropods traveled in coordinated herds: the whole group, they propose, moved across the terrain together at one time."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly undermine Salgado and colleagues' hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "The tracks were all produced by one sauropod species rather than by several different species.", "why": "A single-species assemblage is consistent with a herd of one species, so this supports rather than weakens the interpretation.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Isolated theropod footprints occur elsewhere in the same rock formation.", "why": "The presence of a predator's tracks nearby says nothing about whether the sauropods themselves moved as a coordinated group.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Some trackways were made by smaller individuals whose strides were shorter than those of the largest track-makers.", "why": "A mix of juveniles and adults is consistent with a family herd, so it does not weaken—and could even strengthen—the claim.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "The trackways are impressed into several distinct sediment layers that accumulated over many weeks, showing the prints were not all made at the same time.", "why": "This attacks the specific mechanism of the hypothesis—simultaneous, coordinated movement—by establishing that the animals passed individually over an extended interval rather than together at one time."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-cet-d4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from a critic's discussion of an anonymous 1867 essay on railway travel. The critic argues that, for the essayist, rapid mechanized travel dulls a passenger's attention to the particular places through which the train passes, reducing a varied countryside to an undifferentiated blur."}, "stem": "Which quotation from the essay would best illustrate the critic's claim?", "choices": [{"text": "“What our grandfathers needed three days to accomplish we now dispatch before noon; ours is an age that has learned to husband its hours as no former one did.”", "why": "This celebrates the speed and efficiency of rail travel, the opposite of the essayist's complaint that such travel dulls attention.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "“Borne along at such a pace, the traveler ceases to see the fields at all; hamlet and hedgerow, meadow and mill dissolve into one long smear of green, and he arrives knowing nothing of the country he has crossed.”", "why": "This ties the speed of travel directly to a loss of attention to particular places, which “dissolve into one long smear,” capturing both halves of the critic's claim."}, {"text": "“The carriages are close and smoky, and the incessant shriek of the whistle makes conversation among the passengers next to impossible.”", "why": "This complains about physical discomfort and noise, not about passengers' inattention to the landscape they pass.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "“The old coaching inns, once lively with ostlers and guests, now stand shuttered along the abandoned high roads.”", "why": "This laments the economic decline of roadside establishments, not the passenger's dulled perception of the places the train passes.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-ceq-d1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Nineteenth-century European concert life is often said to have become a \"musical museum,\" as works by living composers gradually ceded programs and publishers' catalogs to a canon of deceased masters. Musicologist Berit Lindqvist argues that this transformation was not synchronized across the continent: drawing on the catalogs of major music publishers in two centers of the trade, she contends that the turn away from living composers' works took hold decades earlier in Leipzig than in Paris. Catalog data support her contention: ______", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Publishers' Catalog Entries Devoted to Works by Living Composers", "xLabel": "Year", "yLabel": "Percent of catalog entries", "categories": ["1840", "1860", "1880", "1900"], "series": [{"name": "Leipzig", "values": [61, 44, 32, 27]}, {"name": "Paris", "values": [79, 68, 55, 41]}], "yFormat": "percent"}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the text?", "choices": [{"text": "in Paris, the share of catalog entries devoted to works by living composers fell from 79 percent in 1840 to 41 percent in 1900.", "why": "Accurately reads the Paris series, but a decline in one city alone cannot show that Leipzig's turn away from living composers happened earlier; Lindqvist's contention requires comparing the timing of the two cities' declines.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "the share of catalog entries devoted to works by living composers had already fallen below 50 percent in Leipzig by 1860, whereas in Paris it did not drop below 50 percent until 1900.", "why": "Lindqvist's contention is comparative and temporal—the shift occurred decades earlier in Leipzig. Only this choice captures that timing gap: Leipzig's share is below 50 percent by 1860 (44 percent), while Paris's remains above 50 percent through 1880 (55 percent) and falls below only in 1900 (41 percent)."}, {"text": "in each year shown, the share of catalog entries devoted to works by living composers was lower in Paris than in Leipzig.", "why": "Reverses the graph: in every year shown, Paris's share (79, 68, 55, and 41 percent) is higher, not lower, than Leipzig's (61, 44, 32, and 27 percent).", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "by 1900, works by living composers accounted for less than one-fifth of catalog entries in both Leipzig and Paris.", "why": "The 1900 values are 27 percent for Leipzig and 41 percent for Paris—both above one-fifth—so this misstates the data, and even if true it would not address the relative timing of the two declines.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-ceq-d2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Hillslopes in tectonically active mountain ranges are thought to approach a \"threshold\" angle: once slopes become steep enough that landslides remove debris about as quickly as bedrock weathering produces it, further steepening should do little to accelerate erosion. To test this idea, geomorphologist Daniela Ibarra and colleagues used beryllium-10 concentrations in river sand to estimate long-term erosion rates for catchments in Taiwan's Central Range, grouping the catchments by average hillslope angle. Ibarra concluded that the catchments' behavior is consistent with a threshold angle near 30 degrees.", "chart": {"type": "bar", "title": "Mean Erosion Rate of Catchments Grouped by Average Hillslope Angle", "xLabel": "Average hillslope angle (degrees)", "yLabel": "Mean erosion rate (mm per 1,000 years)", "categories": ["10", "15", "20", "25", "30", "35"], "series": [{"name": "Mean erosion rate", "values": [40, 75, 150, 310, 520, 540]}], "yFormat": "number", "note": "Rates estimated from beryllium-10 concentrations in river sand."}}, "stem": "Which choice best describes data from the graph that support Ibarra's conclusion?", "choices": [{"text": "Catchments with an average hillslope angle of 35 degrees eroded more than 13 times as fast as catchments with an average hillslope angle of 10 degrees.", "why": "Accurate (540 versus 40 mm per 1,000 years), but a single steep-versus-gentle comparison shows only that steeper catchments erode faster; it says nothing about erosion leveling off near 30 degrees, which is what a threshold requires.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Erosion rates roughly doubled with each 5-degree increase in average hillslope angle across the entire range studied.", "why": "The doubling pattern holds only up to 30 degrees; from 30 to 35 degrees the rate rises just 4 percent (520 to 540 mm per 1,000 years). If rates truly kept doubling across the entire range, that would contradict, not support, a threshold near 30 degrees.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Erosion rates changed little as average hillslope angles increased from 10 to 25 degrees but rose sharply at steeper angles.", "why": "Inverts the graph's pattern: the sharp rise occurs between 10 and 25 degrees (40 to 310 mm per 1,000 years), and it is at the steepest angles—between 30 and 35 degrees—that rates level off.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Erosion rates increased more than tenfold as average hillslope angles rose from 10 to 30 degrees but were nearly identical for catchments averaging 30 and 35 degrees.", "why": "The threshold model predicts rapid gains in erosion with steepening below the critical angle and little gain beyond it. This choice matches both halves of that prediction: rates climb from 40 to 520 mm per 1,000 years between 10 and 30 degrees, then barely change between 30 degrees (520) and 35 degrees (540)."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-inf-b5", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "An influential hypothesis holds that languages acquire basic color terms—single, widely known words not restricted to a particular class of objects—in a fixed sequence: no language, for example, should have a basic term for blue without also having one for red. A recently published grammar of an Amazonian language appears to defy the sequence, listing a basic term for blue but none for red. Linguist Ama Osei notes, however, that the grammar's authors refused to count the language's everyday word for red things as basic because it doubles as the name of a red-feathered macaw—a standard by which the English term orange, which doubles as the name of a fruit, would fail as well. Osei's observation suggests that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the reported violation may be an artifact of how the grammar's authors classified the language's color vocabulary rather than a genuine exception to the hypothesized sequence.", "why": "Osei's comparison shows that the authors' exclusion criterion, applied consistently, would disqualify an uncontroversially basic term (English 'orange'); the language's reported gap for red may therefore reflect the classification standard, not the language—the only hedged conclusion consistent with all the premises."}, {"text": "any language reported to lack a basic color term for red has probably been documented incorrectly.", "why": "Generalizes a critique of one grammar's criterion to every reported counterexample; Osei's observation concerns how this vocabulary was classified, not the reliability of language documentation at large.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "words that double as names of objects, such as the English term orange, should be excluded from inventories of basic color terms.", "why": "Backwards: Osei invokes 'orange' to show that the doubling-as-object-name standard is untenable, since it would disqualify a clearly basic English term; this choice endorses and extends the very standard her comparison undermines.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "the grammar's authors deliberately misclassified the word for red things in order to produce a counterexample to the hypothesis.", "why": "Imputes deception: the passage describes a methodological choice ('refused to count... because it doubles as the name of a red-feathered macaw'), not an intent to manufacture a counterexample.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-inf-b6", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The Indus script, preserved on seals and pottery from cities that flourished in the Indus Valley roughly four thousand years ago, remains undeciphered, and some scholars doubt that it encodes language at all, noting that surviving inscriptions average only about five signs in length. Proponents of the linguistic hypothesis have countered with statistical analyses showing that the script's signs follow one another with the kind of conditional patterning characteristic of written language. Skeptics reply that certain symbol systems no one regards as writing—medieval European heraldry, for example—display comparable patterning. If the skeptics are correct about such systems, then ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "the Indus script is more plausibly interpreted as a system akin to heraldry than as a form of writing.", "why": "The conditional only neutralizes one line of evidence: showing that the patterning is compatible with non-writing systems does not make the non-linguistic interpretation the more plausible one.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the statistical analyses, whatever their other merits, cannot by themselves establish that the Indus script encodes language.", "why": "If systems 'no one regards as writing' display the same conditional patterning, that patterning cannot discriminate writing from non-writing—so the analyses alone cannot establish the linguistic hypothesis; the guarded 'by themselves' is exactly what the premises license."}, {"text": "statistical methods have no legitimate role in investigating whether ancient symbol systems encode language.", "why": "Overshoots the conclusion: the premises show the analyses are not decisive in this case on their own, not that statistical methods are illegitimate for such questions in general.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "medieval European heraldry may itself have functioned as an unrecognized form of writing.", "why": "Contradicts the premise the conditional adopts—heraldry is introduced precisely as a system 'no one regards as writing'; reclassifying it as writing undoes the skeptics' point rather than following from it.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-bnd-d2", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "boundaries", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "wordId": "", "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "In converting a derelict drum factory in São Paulo into the SESC Pompéia leisure center, the Italian-born Brazilian architect Lina Bo Bardi declined to disguise the site's industrial past. Her reasoning was characteristically ______ exposed concrete sheds, with their sawtooth roofs and rough surfaces, already constituted an architecture, one that needed only to be opened to new uses."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "blunt, the factory's", "why": "This creates a comma splice: \"Her reasoning was characteristically blunt\" and \"the factory's exposed concrete sheds...already constituted an architecture\" are both independent clauses, which a comma alone cannot join.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "blunt; that the factory's", "why": "Semicolons do join independent clauses, but \"that the factory's...sheds...already constituted an architecture\" is a subordinate \"that\" clause, leaving a fragment after the semicolon.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "blunt: the factory's", "why": "\"Her reasoning was characteristically blunt\" is an independent clause, and what follows spells out that reasoning; a colon correctly introduces an explanatory independent clause after a complete one."}, {"text": "blunt the factory's", "why": "This fuses two independent clauses with no punctuation at all, producing a run-on sentence.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-fss-c4", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "form-structure-sense", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Ethnomusicologist Dara Wilbourne has documented how each of the shape-note singing communities scattered across the American South maintains, alongside the shared repertoire of the 1844 tunebook The Sacred Harp, ______ own distinct conventions governing tempo, ornamentation, and the order in which song leaders are called."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "their", "why": "'Their' agrees with the nearby plural 'communities,' but that noun is the object of 'of'; the true antecedent is the singular indefinite pronoun 'each,' as the singular verb 'maintains' confirms.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "they're", "why": "'They're' is the contraction of 'they are,' not a possessive, and its plural referent would be wrong in any case.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "its", "why": "The antecedent is the singular indefinite pronoun 'each' (confirmed by the singular verb 'maintains'), so the singular possessive 'its' is required despite the intervening plurals."}, {"text": "it's", "why": "'It's' is the contraction of 'it is'; the sentence requires the possessive form 'its' before 'own distinct conventions.'", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-bnd-d3", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "boundaries", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "wordId": "", "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "To determine whether the \"doorway effect\"—the well-documented tendency for intentions to slip from memory when a person walks through a doorway—arises from physical movement or from the mental updating of one's surroundings, cognitive psychologist Priya Raghunathan tested volunteers in ______ Columbus, Ohio; and Burlington, Vermont, asking each group to carry virtual objects between rooms in an immersive digital environment."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "Tempe, Arizona,", "why": "Commas ordinarily separate list items, but because each item here contains an internal comma, a comma after \"Arizona\" would collapse the two levels of the list, making \"Arizona\" and \"Columbus\" read as parallel items.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Tempe, Arizona;", "why": "Each item in this series contains an internal comma (\"Columbus, Ohio\"), so the items themselves must be separated by semicolons, matching \"Ohio; and Burlington, Vermont.\""}, {"text": "Tempe, Arizona:", "why": "A colon may introduce a list only after a complete clause; it cannot stand between items within a list already under way.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Tempe, Arizona—", "why": "A dash would break the parallel punctuation of the series, whose remaining items are separated by a semicolon, and would falsely signal an interruption.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-fss-c5", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "form-structure-sense", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Geomorphologist Elena Marchetti attributes the formation of the sinuous slot canyons incised into southern Utah's Navajo Sandstone to three interacting processes: the abrasive slurry of sand and boulders that flash floods drive through narrow channels, the slow dissolution of the calcite cement binding the sandstone's grains, and ______."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "when freezing winter meltwater gradually pries apart jointed rock", "why": "An adverbial 'when' clause is not parallel to the noun phrases 'the abrasive slurry...' and 'the slow dissolution...' that name the first two processes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "freezing winter meltwater gradually pries apart jointed rock", "why": "An independent clause cannot serve as the third item in a list of noun phrases standing in apposition to 'three interacting processes.'", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "that freezing winter meltwater gradually pries apart jointed rock", "why": "A 'that' clause is not parallel to the two preceding noun phrases and cannot function as an appositive naming one of the 'processes.'", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the gradual prying apart of jointed rock by freezing winter meltwater", "why": "The list in apposition to 'three interacting processes' requires a third noun phrase parallel to 'the abrasive slurry...' and 'the slow dissolution...'; this choice completes the pattern."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-bnd-d4", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "boundaries", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "wordId": "", "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Completed in 1996, the Eastgate Centre in Harare, Zimbabwe, is often cited as biomimicry's most persuasive built argument: architect Mick Pearce modeled its cooling strategy on the self-ventilating mounds of local termites. The network of concrete chimneys, subfloor cavities, and fans through which the building draws in cool night air and expels the heat its high-mass fabric absorbs during the ______ offices and shops at a comfortable temperature without a conventional air-conditioning plant."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "day, keeps its", "why": "A single comma cannot separate a subject from its verb, no matter how long the intervening modifier (\"through which the building draws...\") makes the subject feel.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "day—keeps its", "why": "A dash signals an interruption, but no paired dash opened an aside within the subject; the mark would sever the subject from its verb.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "day; keeps its", "why": "A semicolon requires an independent clause on each side, but \"keeps its offices and shops...\" has no subject of its own.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "day keeps its", "why": "The long subject \"The network of concrete chimneys, subfloor cavities, and fans...\" must connect directly to its verb \"keeps\"; no punctuation may intervene between a subject and its verb."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-fss-c6", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "form-structure-sense", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Of the ten full-scale cartoons that Raphael and his workshop painted in 1515 and 1516 as designs for a cycle of tapestries commissioned for the Sistine Chapel, seven—each executed in glue-based distemper on dozens of sheets of paper pasted together and later mounted on canvas—______ in London, where the Victoria and Albert Museum has displayed them since 1865."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "survive", "why": "The subject is the plural 'seven' (of the ten cartoons); the dash-enclosed phrase beginning 'each executed...' is a nonessential modifier and does not change the subject's number."}, {"text": "survives", "why": "Singular 'survives' wrongly agrees with 'each' inside the nonessential dash-enclosed phrase rather than with the plural subject 'seven.'", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "has survived", "why": "'Has survived' is singular, but the subject 'seven' is plural; 'each' merely introduces an interrupting modifier between subject and verb.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "surviving", "why": "The nonfinite form 'surviving' leaves the sentence without a main verb, producing a fragment.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-bnd-d5", "domain": "conventions", "skill": "boundaries", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 50, "wordId": "", "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Early theories of visual attention treated fixation as a proxy for awareness: if the eyes land on an object, the mind registers it. Eye-tracking research by cognitive scientist Maarten De Vries complicates that equivalence. In his studies of inattentional blindness, nearly half of the observers whose gaze rested directly on an unexpected shape for at least a quarter of a second nonetheless denied having ______ looking at something is no guarantee of perceiving it."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?", "choices": [{"text": "seen it, therefore,", "why": "This is a comma splice: a conjunctive adverb such as \"therefore\" cannot join two independent clauses with commas alone.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "seen it, therefore;", "why": "This swaps the required marks, putting the comma before \"therefore\" and the semicolon after it, so the semicolon severs \"therefore\" from the clause it introduces.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "seen it; therefore,", "why": "Two independent clauses linked by the conjunctive adverb \"therefore\" require a semicolon before the adverb and a comma after it."}, {"text": "seen it therefore,", "why": "With no punctuation before \"therefore,\" the two independent clauses simply run together in a fused sentence.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-tra-b4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from Kate Chopin's 1894 short story \"The Story of an Hour.\"\n\nAt first Louise Mallard sat stunned; then she wept at once, with sudden, wild abandonment, in her sister's arms. Retreating alone to her room, she sank into an armchair as a nameless thing reached toward her out of the open window—an approach too subtle and too strong to be beaten back by her will. ______ she abandoned any pretense of resistance and let a whispered word escape her lips—\"free, free, free!\"—recognizing in the news of her husband's death not only sorrow but the dawn of a life that would be wholly her own."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Nevertheless,", "why": "This marks a reversal of expectation, but sentence 2 says the feeling was 'too strong to be beaten back,' so yielding to it is expected, not contrary.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "For instance,", "why": "Yielding is not an example of the approaching feeling described in sentence 2; it is the next and final moment of the sequence.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Ultimately,", "why": "The text narrates escalating stages ('At first... then... reached toward her... too strong to be beaten back'); ceasing resistance and speaking the word is the final stage of that process, which a culmination transition marks."}, {"text": "By comparison,", "why": "Nothing is being compared; the sentence completes a single narrative progression, so a comparison transition does not fit.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-tra-b5", "domain": "expression", "skill": "transitions", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When English absorbed a wave of Norman French vocabulary after 1066, it often kept the native Anglo-Saxon term for a living animal—\"cow,\" \"pig,\" \"sheep\"—while adopting a French-derived word for its cooked flesh: \"beef,\" \"pork,\" \"mutton.\" The division mirrored a social one, separating those who tended the herds from those who dined on them. ______ Japanese pairs native \"kun\" readings of its characters, used in everyday speech, with borrowed Sino-Japanese \"on\" readings reserved for learned and technical vocabulary."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?", "choices": [{"text": "Likewise,", "why": "Sentences 1-2 describe English's split between animal names and meat names; sentence 3 presents Japanese's kun/on split as a second language showing the same kind of stratification, calling for a comparison transition."}, {"text": "However,", "why": "The Japanese pattern parallels the English one rather than contrasting with it, so a contrast transition reverses the relationship.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "For example,", "why": "Sentences 1-2 describe only English and state no general rule, so Japanese cannot be an instance of it; it is a separate language shown to behave similarly.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "In conclusion,", "why": "Sentence 3 introduces a new example rather than summing up the passage, so a concluding transition does not fit.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-rhs-c4", "domain": "expression", "skill": "rhetorical-synthesis", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 60, "passage": {"type": "notes", "intro": "While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:", "bullets": ["The familiar essay is a literary genre consisting of short, personal essays written in an informal, conversational tone.", "English author Charles Lamb published a collection of such essays, Essays of Elia, in 1823.", "Lamb wrote the essays under the pen name \"Elia.\"", "The essays treat ordinary subjects, such as card games and a favorite roast dinner.", "The essays combine gentle humor with autobiographical reflection.", "Literary scholars consider Lamb one of the great practitioners of the familiar essay."]}, "stem": "The student wants to introduce Charles Lamb's Essays of Elia to an audience unfamiliar with the familiar essay. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?", "choices": [{"text": "Charles Lamb published Essays of Elia in 1823 under the pen name \"Elia.\"", "why": "Supplies publication facts but never explains what a familiar essay is, which an unfamiliar audience needs.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Literary scholars consider Charles Lamb one of the great practitioners of the familiar essay.", "why": "Asserts Lamb's stature in the genre without defining the genre itself, so an unfamiliar audience learns nothing about what a familiar essay is.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "Charles Lamb's Essays of Elia (1823) are a collection of familiar essays—short, personal pieces written in an informal, conversational tone.", "why": "Defines the familiar essay ('short, personal essays written in an informal, conversational tone') and identifies Lamb's work as an example, exactly what an unfamiliar audience requires."}, {"text": "Charles Lamb's Essays of Elia treat ordinary subjects, such as card games and a favorite roast dinner, with gentle humor.", "why": "Describes the essays' subjects and tone but never defines the familiar essay as a genre, leaving an unfamiliar audience without the key concept.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 2}]}
);
